## Swift

### Swift-Guideline

- Follow the Swift Package Manager layout: each target's sources under `Sources/<Target>/` and its tests under `Tests/<Target>Tests/`; follow the project's existing layout where it already uses another, such as an Xcode project's groups.
- Keep the entry point (`main.swift` or the `@main` type) thin: build the collaborators, inject them, start.
- Keep `Package.swift` declarative: products, targets, and dependencies.
- Split code into targets along domain lines, so `internal` access enforces the boundaries between them; never grow one target that every feature edits.
<!-- Project-specific / Swift-Guideline -->

### Swift-Layout

```
├── <App>.xcodeproj/ # Xcode project (Apple-platform apps; optional)
├── Plugins/         # SwiftPM build-tool and command plugins (optional)
├── Sources/         # one folder per target (Sources/<Target>/); an executable target holds main.swift or the @main type
├── Tests/           # one folder per test target (Tests/<Target>Tests/), mirroring Sources/
├── docs/            # design and user documents
├── examples/        # examples for applications and/or public libraries
├── scripts/         # build/install/analysis scripts
├── .swift-format    # swift-format configuration (optional)
├── .swift-version   # pinned Swift toolchain (optional)
├── .swiftlint.yml   # SwiftLint configuration (optional)
├── Dockerfile       # container build definition
├── Makefile         # build/test/lint task automation (optional)
├── Package.resolved # dependency lockfile (committed for applications)
├── Package.swift    # package manifest: products, targets, and dependencies
<!-- Project-specific / Swift-Layout -->
```

### Swift-Layer Dependencies

Notes for declaring architecture layers in the `Layer Dependencies` section of `.attunic/context.md`; adjust them to the project before declaring.

- The executable target (`main.swift` or the `@main` type) is the outermost architecture layer and the composition root: the only place that constructs a concrete adapter and injects it; it may import everything, and nothing imports it.
- Architecture layers are SwiftPM targets, each listing in its `dependencies` only the targets its layer may depend on; one suggestion, innermost first:

| Architecture layer | Typical path | Depends on | Must never contain |
|--------------------|--------------|------------|--------------------|
| domain | Sources/Domain | itself | UI frameworks (`SwiftUI`, `UIKit`, `AppKit`), networking, persistence |
| application | Sources/Application | domain | database access, network calls, UI |
| adapters | Sources/Adapters | application (its ports), domain | business rules |
| main | Sources/\<App\> | application, adapters, domain | business rules |

- Declare ports as protocols in the domain or application target; adapter targets conform to them, and the composition root injects them through initializers.
- Check manually: in `Package.swift`, an inner target's `dependencies` must never list an outer target; then read the `import`s of changed files, since a build can resolve an import of an already-built target that its own target doesn't list.
